\details{
\itemize{
\item \code{robust_outliers()}: the default of the package. An observation is an
outlier when its change from the previous period and the next change
are both more than \code{threshold} robust z-scores (deviation from the
median change divided by the median absolute deviation) and of
opposite sign, that is, a one-period spike. Spikes are replaced by
linear interpolation. An extreme first change is replaced too (the
first value becomes the second minus the median change); the last
observation is left alone, because an extreme last change cannot yet
be told apart from a level shift or a turn. No model and no external
program are needed.
\item \code{tramo_outliers()}: the OECD treatment (Gyomai and Guidetti 2012,
p. 6--7). A regARIMA model with automatic outlier detection finds
additive outliers (AO), transitory changes (TC) and level shifts (LS);
the estimated effects of the types in \code{remove} are subtracted from the
series, which replaces the outlying observations by their estimated
values. The same model provides \code{forecast} periods of forecasts that
extend the series before the cycle filter, which stabilises the end of
the cycle. With \code{engine = "tramo"} the model is TRAMO, through the
\pkg{RJDemetra} package (JDemetra+, needs Java), as at the OECD; with
\code{engine = "x13"} it is the regARIMA part of X-13ARIMA-SEATS through
\pkg{seasonal}, which uses the same outlier types and detection
procedure (Chen and Liu 1993); \code{"auto"} takes TRAMO when \pkg{RJDemetra}
is installed and X-13 otherwise.
}

By default only additive outliers (AO) are removed, as the OECD
describes its practice (Gyomai, Ahmad and Astolfi 2017, p. 283: additive
outliers are filtered, level shifts are analysed and removed only on
expert judgement; ADR-010). Transitory changes (TC) and level shifts
(LS) are detected, so that they are not mistaken for other outliers,
and reported, but kept: in monthly activity data the automatic
procedure often labels the onset of a recession a level shift or a
transitory change, and removing it would erase a genuine cyclical
movement. \code{remove = c("AO", "TC")} was the default before 0.2.0;
\code{remove = c("AO", "TC", "LS")} gives the fully linearised series (Gyomai
and Guidetti 2012, p. 6).

\strong{An AO and a TC on the same date.} Their regressors are nearly
collinear: after first differencing they correlate
\eqn{(2 - r)\sqrt{1 + r}/2}{(2 - r) sqrt(1 + r) / 2}, 0.96 for the
quarterly X-13 decay rate \eqn{r = 0.343}{r = 0.343} and 0.85 for
\eqn{r = 0.7}{r = 0.7}. The model then cannot tell how the deviation at
that date splits between them, and the two estimates can be large with
opposite signs; removing only the AO would leave the TC's value at that
date in the series, a deviation the data never showed, and can date a
false turning point. cyclind therefore rewrites the pair, without
changing the fitted effect, as an AO equal to the whole deviation at that
date (\eqn{a + c}) and a TC that starts one period later (\eqn{c r}):
with \code{remove = "AO"} the deviation at that date is removed and the
transitory change after it is kept. A warning of class
\code{cyclind_warning_outlier_collinear} names the date and the correlation,
the \code{collinear} column of the outlier table and of \code{\link[=outlier_report]{outlier_report()}}
marks the pair, and the \code{steps} record it (ADR-012). Pairs on adjacent
dates are not rewritten: each regressor then owns a date of its own.
Several regARIMA programs may also pick different models for the same
series (for example X-13 levels with ARIMA(1 2 0), TRAMO logarithms);
setting \code{transform} of the specification fixes the scale for both.

Every treatment reports the outliers it found: \code{\link[=cli_cycles]{cli_cycles()}} keeps
their dates and types in the \code{outliers} slot of each
\linkS4class{CycleSeries}, \code{\link[=outlier_report]{outlier_report()}} tabulates them, and the
\code{show()} methods print them. When the outliers removed from a series
exceed \code{max_share} of its observations, a warning of class
\code{cyclind_warning_outlier_share} is given and recorded in the \code{notes}
slot: so many replaced values change the cycle itself, and the series or
the threshold deserve a look.

When X-13ARIMA-SEATS cannot estimate the model, the series keeps its
outliers, a warning of class \code{cyclind_warning_x13} names the series and
the error, and the \code{notes} slot records it. Errors of the system (a full
disk, a missing X-13 binary, a file that cannot be written) stop the run
with an error of class \code{cyclind_error_system}.

\strong{Expert decisions.} The OECD "detects and filters out 'additive'
outliers (temporary shocks), and analyses 'level shift' outliers
(permanent shocks) but does not necessarily filter these out; it is based
on expert judgement whether level shifts are eliminated or not", and
when "an extreme event triggers a whole set of changes in economic
activity the rule of removing outliers is more ambiguous" (Gyomai, Ahmad
and Astolfi 2017, p. 283). The \code{decisions} table of \code{tramo_outliers()}
records such judgements one series and one date at a time: an outlier
found by the model in that series, at that date and of that type, is
removed or kept as the table says, whatever \code{remove} says for its type;
every other outlier follows \code{remove}. For example, the level shift of
April 2020 can be removed from the reference only, and kept in the
candidates where it is a genuine cyclical movement. A decision needs a
reason. A decision that matches no outlier found by the model (another
date or type, or a failed model) has no effect and gives a warning of
class \code{cyclind_warning_outlier_decision} that lists the outliers found;
so does a decision for a series that is not in the data. The decisions
cannot add an outlier that the model did not find. The effect removed is
the estimated coefficient times the regressor of the outlier (for an AO
and a TC on the same date, their rewritten effects, see above), also in
the forecasts. The \code{steps} of the cycle record the decisions applied,
and \code{\link[=expert_log]{expert_log()}} lists them with their status.

New treatments are added by defining a subclass and a method for the
generic \code{treat_outliers()}, which returns a list with the cleaned series
\code{x}, the number of outliers \code{n}, a description \code{step}, a numeric vector
\code{forecast} (possibly empty) and, optionally, a data frame \code{table} of the
outliers found (\code{index}, \code{type}, \code{removed}, optionally \code{collinear}) and a
character \code{failed} (the error message when the model failed).
}
